 "fullarm": "Context from a materials paper:\n- High-temperature dielectric films need higher permittivity with low loss while keeping polymer strength and thermal stability\n- BST core carrying an MPTMS thiol layer decorated with Ag nanoparticles, used as hybrid filler\n- H2O2 hydroxylation of BST then MPTMS hydrolysis/coupling gives thiol-grafted BST-SH\n- Alcohol-thermal reduction of AgNO3 in ethylene glycol with PVP at 160 C deposits Ag on BST-SH\n- Ag nanoparticles coat the BST-SH surface, roughening particles grown to about 100 nm\n- Composite permittivity rises with Ag content, to about 1.8 times pure PAEN at 5% Ag\n- Dielectric properties stay stable from 100 Hz to 1 MHz and up to about 135-140 C\n\nPanels:\n- F6b: caption: Fig. 6 Mechanical properties of pure PAEN and BST @ Ag/PAEN composites ... }$ breaking elongation\n\nQuestion: The text claims that dielectric properties stay stable from 100 Hz to 1 MHz (and up to about 135-140 C) for the BST@Ag/PAEN films. Does panel F6b support this stability claim across that frequency range?\n\nImages (read each with Read):\n/home/aid1/Documents/causalmat/matmech/Advanced_Composites_and_Hybrid_Materials/s42114-021-00366-2/panels/crops/e882a07abadc8f5d206ee50b35b69ee2a96baff78bb55670e0d0aec7725834ce_B.jpg\nAnswer in at most six sentences.",
